  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "CFR-01",
      "title": "Why the good model needs a congruence at an auxiliary principal prime",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-inert-frobenius",
      "statement": "A prescribed inertia element at the target agrees on the roots of an integral monic polynomial for J and in the Artin class quotient with an arithmetic Frobenius φ at a degree-one auxiliary prime ideal 𝔮. The trivial Artin class of inertia makes 𝔮 principal. At 𝔮, φ(J)≡J^q≡J. Avoiding the finite exceptional set where roots collide then forces equality in characteristic zero and hence inertia fixes J.",
      "hypotheses": "d squarefree, d≡3 mod 4, the complex period pair has CM by the quadratic generator and J is its j-invariant realized in the algebraic closure of K=ℚ(√−d) inside ℂ. The chosen auxiliary 𝔮 has prime residue cardinal q, avoids the separation set, and admits an arithmetic Frobenius in the required joint coset. Good-model use fixes an inert rational p≥5, p∤d, and an embedding.",
      "definitions": [
        "auxiliary prime ideal of norm q",
        "joint stabilizer of the roots and Artin kernel",
        "tested inertia element at the target place",
        "root separation outside a finite set"
      ],
