    {
      "id": "CFR-02",
      "title": "The repeated Fourier factors produce a square at a CM point",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-inert-frobenius-congruence",
      "statement": "For any principal prime ideal 𝔮 of the quadratic field with prime norm q, the CM endomorphism of determinant q fixes the period point. A factor of G_q vanishes. At that point (J^q−J)^2=(1−ζ_q)r with r integral, and (J^q−J)^(2q)=q r₀ r^q with r₀ integral. Thus J^q−J reduces to zero at every place over 𝔮.",
      "hypotheses": "Same CM and squarefree discriminant hypotheses as CFR-01; for this congruence q is prime and 𝔮 has trivial ideal class. No root separation or choice of Frobenius is needed for this one congruence. The example d=23, α=5+2ω, basis (τ,1) with τ=ω gives rows (7,−12),(2,5) and determinant 59.",
      "definitions": [
        "norm form a²+ab+((d+1)/4)b²",
        "upper-half-plane parameter",
        "index-q representatives",
        "Fourier series of E₄³ and Δ",
        "cyclotomic coefficient ring ℤ[ζ_q]"
      ],
