    {
      "id": "CFR-03",
      "title": "Why the inert Frobenius has to choose the negative square root",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-inert-frobenius-sign",
      "statement": "For δ=1−2ω, δ²=−d and p inert, Euler's criterion gives δ^p≡−δ. In the completed unramified field an arithmetic Frobenius lift fixes δ² and must carry δ to one of ±δ. Their reductions differ by the unit 2δ, so σδ=−δ, σω=1−ω, σω−ω is a unit and σ²ω=ω.",
      "hypotheses": "p≥5 in the application (the quadratic lemma requires p prime and odd), p∤d, −d is nonsquare in 𝔽_p, d≡3 mod 4, and the chosen ω solves the indicated integral quadratic in ℂ_p. FrobField means that σ lifts the p-power on the residue field, with the stronger norm inequality from the source.",
      "definitions": [
        "coefficient Frobenius on the completed unramified field",
        "the unit discriminant square root",
        "arithmetic Frobenius on the residue field",
        "integral CM action in the height-two logarithm comparison"
      ],
